{"id": "00000ba8-0000-4000-8000-000000000ba8", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll audit the Lambda functions in us-east-1 against serverless best practices. I'll check runtime, memory and timeout, concurrency, DLQs and async config, VPC and tracing settings, log retention, and the other usual areas. I'll also track how much I could actually verify, so the confidence rating rests on real coverage.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_ErqHWCu6AUft1l5WrJUBiA", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "lambda-best-practices-audit", "agent_type": "generic", "preloaded_skills": ["exploring-metrics"], "prompt": "{
  "task": "Audit all AWS Lambda functions in us-east-1 against serverless best practices, read-only. Return a concise findings summary plus an explicit coverage/confidence assessment.",
  "context": "User asked: 'Audit the Lambda functions in us-east-1 against serverless best practices and tell me how confident I should be in the result.' The agent space lives in account 123456789012 (us-east-1). Start with that account; also check list_associations for other associated AWS accounts and cover any that include us-east-1 Lambda functions, reporting per account. Use only read-only API calls (list/get/describe). Paginate fully through list_functions so no function is missed.",
  "resources": ["AWS account 123456789012", "region us-east-1", "all Lambda functions"],
  "timeframe": "Configuration: current state. Metrics: last 14 days ending 2026-11-17T20:00:00Z (use for errors, throttles, duration vs timeout, concurrency), if CloudWatch access works.",
  "criteria": "Check each function (and aggregate by finding, listing function names) for: (1) deprecated/near-EOL runtimes (verify against current AWS docs via verify_aws_claim, don't rely on memory); (2) memory/timeout settings (default 128MB, timeouts at 3s default or at max 900s, timeout vs observed p99 duration); (3) architecture (x86 vs arm64/Graviton opportunity); (4) reserved/provisioned concurrency and throttles; (5) async failure handling: DLQ or on-failure destinations, event invoke config retries/max age; for SQS event source mappings check batch size, partial batch response (ReportBatchItemFailures), and queue visibility timeout >= 6x function timeout, redrive/DLQ on the queue; (6) X-Ray/active tracing; (7) VPC config concerns (functions in VPC without need, single-AZ subnets, missing endpoints); (8) execution role risk: wildcard/AdministratorAccess or overly broad managed policies (read-only get_role/list_attached_role_policies, get_role_policy; do not change anything); (9) env vars: secrets in plaintext (look for key names like PASSWORD/SECRET/TOKEN/KEY - report names only, NEVER values), KMS encryption config; (10) log group retention (never-expire), log group existence, logging config (JSON format); (11) function URLs with AuthType NONE; resource policies allowing Principal '*'; (12) packaging: code size, layers count and outdated layer versions, container vs zip; (13) SnapStart/cold-start considerations for Java; (14) tagging; (15) code signing, if relevant; (16) error rate and throttle anomalies from CloudWatch metrics. Prioritize findings by severity (High/Medium/Low) with counts and example function names.\n\nIMPORTANT - confidence assessment: at the end, report (a) total functions found vs. functions actually inspected for each check, (b) any API calls that were denied, errored, or skipped (permissions gaps, throttling, missing metrics), (c) what could NOT be assessed from configuration alone (application code quality, idempotency, secrets in code, business-criticality, intended traffic patterns, cost), (d) data freshness and any functions with no invocations in the window (metrics-based checks not meaningful), (e) any other accounts/regions not covered, (f) which findings are verified facts versus heuristics/judgment calls. Do not guess or fabricate; state clearly where data was unavailable."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Audit us-east-1 Lambda functions against best practices"}], "created_at": null}

